Only review this package after having finished reviewing 'Inheitance' & 'Inheritance_using_TestNG' packages.

Assume we have the following two .java class files:

********************** ps.java  whose content is:


   public class ps {

     public void doThis() 
     {
      System.out.println("I am here!");
     }
}


 ********************** and ps2.java  whose content is:


 public class ps2 extends ps {
	
	  @Test
	   public void testRun()
	     {
	       doThis();
	     }
	     
	     @BeforeMethod()
	     public void statement()
	     {
	       System.out.println("run before each and every single method");
	     }
         
	     
	     @Test
		   public void gladiator()
		     {
	    	 System.out.println("some method named gladiator");
		     }

      }

// Now, it is a better idea to move the procedural methods (@Before/@After suite,class, method) + utilities that
   are usually used for clean-up, tear-down, launch the browsers, maximize window (= do something before/after)
   , use the calender, fetch/format the time etc. to its parent class that is inherited from, so that the child 
   class that extends it focuses on the actual logic and actual functionality.
   
   This is especially true for visibility & organizational purposes (Ex: If the @Before/@After isn't in the
   base (parent) class, then how will one locate it especially that you're going to have more than one child
   class that extends it ?.
   
   Also, this is one important interview question: 
   Question:
   How does the original code (shown above in ps.java & ps2.java) compare to the one where the boiler plate
   code (represented by the @After/ @Before methods) is moved to the base (parent) class ?
   
   Answer:
   There should be no difference at all. Their execution should yield the same output (Try it for yourself
   by running the .java classes situated under the package this file is under).
